From 1c4204d9b726b943ae87e49198e5de462d832766 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Mon, 22 Jun 2026 18:24:44 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 03499/11362] [outer-loop] Correct program-gen CLAIM B: L2 wall
 is mechanic-shift, not hidden-state (adversarial-review fix)

A hostile adversarial sub-agent reviewed the prior commit (0134adf6c) and REFUTED its
CLAIM B ("deepening walls on hidden state / non-idempotent-reset parity"). Reading the
actual tu93.py env source, it showed the L2 death is DETERMINISTIC (step 3 every trial,
not run-dependent) with the move budget UNEXHAUSTED (46/50) -> not parity, not budget.
Independently re-measured and confirmed: death_steps [3,3,3,3], budget 50.

Corrected finding: the L2 wall is a MODEL-GENERALIZATION failure -- tu93's levels are not
mechanically identical (L2 adds a sprite pixel-validation + rotation state machine calling
lose()), so the L1-induced blocking rule plans an L2-fatal move. This is the precise cost
the Executable-World-Models lever pays: re-induce per mechanic. CLAIM A (L1 planned in
imagination + fresh-env reproduced) was reviewed and SURVIVES unchanged.

- Replaced the non-sequitur "any game-over => hidden_state_bound" classifier with
  diagnose_deepening_stall(): empirically measures L2 death-step determinism + budget over
  fresh-env replays and reports diagnosed_cause, instead of asserting one.
- Corrected research note (with explicit correction banner), known-issues .425 fold, and
  changelog. tu93 artifact re-run; adversarial_verify clean.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
